Odd-Length Example

Input:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5

First swap:

2 → 1 → 3 → 4 → 5

Second swap:

2 → 1 → 4 → 3 → 5

Now:

prev.next = 5
prev.next.next = null

So the loop stops.

Final:

2 → 1 → 4 → 3 → 5

The last node stays unchanged.

Why Do We Need a Dummy Node?

Without a dummy node, the first swap is special because the head changes.

For:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4

after swapping the first pair:

2 → 1 → 3 → 4
↑
new head

A dummy node removes this special case:

dummy → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4

After swapping:

dummy → 2 → 1 → 3 → 4

Therefore:

return dummy.next;

always gives us the correct head.

Approach 4 — Most Optimal Iterative Solution ⭐

The iterative pointer manipulation is already asymptotically optimal.

Your code is the best approach:

class Solution {
    public ListNode swapPairs(ListNode head) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;

        ListNode prev = dummy;

        while (prev.next != null && prev.next.next != null) {

            ListNode first = prev.next;
            ListNode second = first.next;

            first.next = second.next;
            second.next = first;
            prev.next = second;

            prev = first;
        }

        return dummy.next;
    }
}

Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(1)

No recursion, no array, no stack.

Complexity Comparison
Approach	Technique	Time	Extra Space	Actually swaps nodes?
1	Swap values	O(n)	O(1)	❌
2	Recursion	O(n)	O(n)	✅
3	Iterative pointers	O(n)	O(1)	✅
4	Dummy + iterative pointers	O(n)	O(1)	✅
Most Important Pointer Pattern

The key pattern is:

Before:

prev → first → second → next

We want:

After:

prev → second → first → next

The three pointer updates are:

first.next = second.next;
second.next = first;
prev.next = second;

Then:

prev = first;

This is the main thing to remember for interviews.

